From: =?UTF-8?q?Juan=20Manuel=20L=C3=B3pez=20Carrillo?=
 <juanmanuellopezcarrillo@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 23:10:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 135/143] arm64: dts: allwinner: t527-orangepi-4a: give the
 little cluster OPPs a voltage range

DCDC1 (vdd-cpul) does not power the little cluster alone.  The DSU --
the DynamIQ Shared Unit, i.e. the coherent interconnect and the 1 MB
shared L3 -- hangs off the very same rail, and the vendor board file
wires it there explicitly (dsu-supply = <&reg_dcdc1>).  Nothing in
mainline drives the DSU today, so it sits at whatever the firmware
left it at, but a driver that scales it has to raise this rail.

That is impossible while every OPP names an exact voltage.
opp_parse_supplies() turns a single opp-microvolt value into
u_volt_min == u_volt_max, so each cpufreq transition pins the rail to
one point.  regulator_check_consumers() then aggregates the rail as
max-of-mins and min-of-maxes across consumers, so a second consumer
asking for anything higher makes min exceed max and the transition
fails with -EINVAL ("Restricting voltage").

Declare a range instead.  The target and lower bound stay exactly as
before, so this is a no-op with a single consumer: the core still
picks the lowest voltage satisfying the request.  The upper bound is
1.15 V, the highest voltage the vendor tables ever put on this rail
(the cluster's own 1.416 GHz point), so the aggregate can never
exceed a voltage the vendor already sanctions here.

Measured on the board, the DSU is worth coordinating: raising it from
696 to 936 MHz cuts dependent-load latency in the 256-512 KB working
set by 20-22% and makes JIT-style cache maintenance 20% cheaper,
while L1 latency is unchanged as expected.

Co-Authored-By: AI Agent
---
 .../dts/allwinner/sun55i-t527-orangepi-4a.dts | 21 ++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun55i-t527-orangepi-4a.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun55i-t527-orangepi-4a.dts
index 5a1c38c0d..f6732935b 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun55i-t527-orangepi-4a.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun55i-t527-orangepi-4a.dts
@@ -249,33 +249,44 @@ / {
 	 * 0.90 V sits within the vendor envelope (408 and 672 MHz both run
 	 * at 0.90 V on every speed bin).
 	 */
+	/*
+	 * DCDC1 (vdd-cpul) does not power this cluster alone: the DSU (the
+	 * DynamIQ Shared Unit, i.e. the coherent interconnect and the shared
+	 * L3) hangs off the very same rail. Each OPP therefore declares a
+	 * range instead of an exact voltage. opp_parse_supplies() turns a
+	 * single value into u_volt_min == u_volt_max, and with that
+	 * regulator_check_consumers() aggregates to min > max and fails the
+	 * transition with -EINVAL the moment the other consumer of the rail
+	 * asks for a different voltage. The upper bound is 1.15 V, the
+	 * highest voltage the vendor tables ever put on this rail.
+	 */
 	cluster0_opp: opp-table-cluster0 {
 		compatible = "operating-points-v2";
 		opp-shared;
 
 		opp-480000000 {
 			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <480000000>;
-			opp-microvolt = <900000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <900000 900000 1150000>;
 		};
 
 		opp-792000000 {
 			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <792000000>;
-			opp-microvolt = <900000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <900000 900000 1150000>;
 		};
 
 		opp-1008000000 {
 			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1008000000>;
-			opp-microvolt = <960000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <960000 960000 1150000>;
 		};
 
 		opp-1224000000 {
 			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1224000000>;
-			opp-microvolt = <1050000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <1050000 1050000 1150000>;
 		};
 
 		opp-1416000000 {
 			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1416000000>;
-			opp-microvolt = <1150000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <1150000 1150000 1150000>;
 		};
 	};
 
